# Every brew Invocation Kills The Sudo Ticket

`brew` unconditionally invalidates the caller's sudo timestamp. From
`brew.sh` in the Homebrew library:

```
# Reset sudo timestamp to avoid running unauthorized sudo commands
if [[ -n "${SUDO}" ]]
then
  "${SUDO}" --reset-timestamp 2>/dev/null || true
fi
```

No guard, no opt-out, no env var. Bisected live in a clean macOS 26.6.1 VM on
2026-08-08: ticket VALID after `sudo -v`, VALID after `brew --version` (that
one short-circuits earlier), DEAD after `brew update --force --quiet`.

**Why it matters:** the standard "authenticate once, then refresh with
`sudo -n true` every 60 s" keep-alive cannot survive this. The keep-alive
itself is fine (measured VALID at t+5min and t+6min, past the 5-minute
`timestamp_timeout`), but nothing can re-mint a ticket after `-k` without the
password again. Any unattended script that runs `brew` and then needs root
will stop on a second password prompt. The Homebrew *installer*'s
`trap '/usr/bin/sudo -k' EXIT` is a red herring; it is guarded by
`! sudo -n -v` and does not fire when a ticket is held.

**How to apply:** for a one-password-then-unattended run that touches Homebrew
(including `brew bundle` from nix-darwin activation), do not park the
authorization in a ticket. After the single successful `sudo -v`, install a
NOPASSWD drop-in under `/etc/sudoers.d/`, validate it with `visudo -cqf`
before installing it 0440 root:wheel, and release it from an EXIT/INT/TERM
trap; have the next run reinstall and release the same path so a SIGKILLed run
cannot leak the grant. Implemented in this repo's `install.sh`
(`sudo_grant_acquire` / `sudo_grant_release`, commit e3fa548).
